[38;5;252m[1m## One real defect found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintIntPtrLine[0m used a [38;5;10m*intNumber[0m deref. In [1mrelease[22m that matches C, but in [1mdebug[22m rustc's UB checks fire on the dereference and turn a panic into [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| input | C | Rust ([38;5;10m*p[0m, debug) | Rust ([38;5;10mptr::read[0m) |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| [38;5;10mNULL[0m | SIGSEGV | SIGABRT ✗ | SIGSEGV ✓ |[0m[0m
| misaligned [38;5;10mint*[0m | prints value | SIGABRT ✗ | prints value ✓ |[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Switching to [38;5;10mstd::ptr::read(intNumber)[0m fixes both. Release codegen is unchanged — [38;5;10mmov (%rdi),%esi[0m then tail-call [38;5;10mprintf[0m, with the format string bytes [38;5;10m25 64 0a 00[0m identical to C's. This also removed latent flakiness on the [38;5;10mbad()[0m path, where a garbage pointer that happened to be misaligned would have aborted in debug instead of faulting.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## [38;5;10mbad()[0m cannot be byte-compared, by construction[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mbad()[0m dereferences an uninitialized pointer, so it prints whatever the [3mcaller[23m left in one stack slot. The same C [38;5;10m.so[0m behaves two different ways: called directly it prints garbage and returns; via [38;5;10mdriver(0)[0m it segfaults, because the read slot lands below the caller's frame. Rather than assert on uninitialized memory, [38;5;10mtests/undefined_behaviour.rs[0m pins the part that is well defined — each run either faults or emits exactly one [38;5;10m"%d\n"[0m line — and adds an [38;5;10mobjdump[0m cross-check that both implementations load 8 bytes from an unwritten frame slot and never store to it first. This catches the failure modes that [3mwould[23m be translation bugs: the call being optimized away, duplicated, or replaced by a Rust panic.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Harness design[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Every call runs in a freshly [38;5;10mexec[0med child that [38;5;10mdlopen[0ms one library and invokes it via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m — Rust is never called directly, so the [38;5;10m#[no_mangle][0m wrappers are under test. I moved to subprocess isolation after two in-process attempts failed: redirecting fd 1 inside the test process races against libtest's writes from other threads (this produced a genuine false failure where libtest's own [38;5;10m"test ... ok"[0m text landed in the captured bytes). Children also survive the UB crashes. Two guards prevent vacuous passes: the worker exits with code 42 so a child that never ran isn't mistaken for "empty output matches empty output", and [38;5;10mrust_lib_path[0m rejects a [38;5;10m.so[0m older than [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m, since [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does not rebuild a [38;5;10mcdylib[0m-only lib.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Coverage runs bottom-up: [38;5;10mprintIntPtrLine[0m (digit-width boundaries, [38;5;10mi32::MIN/MAX[0m, mid-array and misaligned pointers, 2000-line runs crossing the [38;5;10mstdio[0m buffer) → [38;5;10mgood()[0m → [38;5;10mdriver()[0m (including selectors like [38;5;10m0x100[0m and [38;5;10mi32::MIN[0m, which would take the wrong branch if truthiness were narrowed). Both libraries import [38;5;10mprintf[0m from [38;5;10mlibc.so.6[0m and bundle no [38;5;10mstdio[0m, which I verified behaviorally by interleaving the caller's own [38;5;10mprintf[0m with library calls.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat worth stating: the UB-path tests confirm both sides stay within the same behavioral envelope, but they do not — and cannot — prove byte-identical output for [38;5;10mbad()[0m or [38;5;10mdriver(0)[0m. If your grading harness byte-compares those, expect divergence from the C itself run under a different caller.[0m[0m
